Use the data protection keychain and PasscodeCore's access groups on macOS.

Why: upstream keeps macOS items in the file-based login keychain, whose
ACL admits only the binary that created an item, so TelegramShare.appex
cannot read the passcode the app stored (errSecAuthFailed; Task 1 of
docs/plans/20261017-passcode-core.md). The data protection keychain
shares items through a keychain-access-groups entitlement instead.

Upstream lines (PasscodeCore/Sources/Passcode.swift, release-13.0.0):
- PasscodeKeychain.query (:126-128) set kSecAttrAccessGroup only
  `#if !os(macOS)`. Now every platform sets it (sharedAccessGroup(),
  privateAccessGroup() for `biometric.` items) and macOS also sets
  kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain. read, write (incl. the biometric
  .biometryCurrentSet item) and remove all build on this query.
- sharedAccessGroup() returns PasscodeConfiguration.appGroupIdentifier
  verbatim, so on macOS the app passes the keychain group there
  (`<team>.com.korthane.telex.shared`), not the app group.

Left alone on purpose:
- adoptGroupedItem (:166-184) now repeats the main query and finds
  nothing new; it runs only without a keychainScope, which Telex sets.
- PasscodeKeychainScope.migrateLegacyItems still copies login-keychain
  items into the scoped login-keychain names, where the patched query no
  longer looks. Telex has no such legacy items and never calls it.

Tests: query assertions for the new attributes; the two round trips
through the real keychain skip without the entitlement (swift test).

diff --git a/submodules/PasscodeCore/Sources/Passcode.swift b/submodules/PasscodeCore/Sources/Passcode.swift
index d1ff0f651b..b177d88bb8 100644
--- a/submodules/PasscodeCore/Sources/Passcode.swift
+++ b/submodules/PasscodeCore/Sources/Passcode.swift
@@ -123,9 +123,11 @@ final class PasscodeKeychain: PasscodeStorage {
             kSecAttrAccount as String: account,
             kSecAttrSynchronizable as String: false
         ]
-        #if !os(macOS)
-        query[kSecAttrAccessGroup as String] = try account.hasPrefix("biometric.") ? self.environment.privateAccessGroup() : self.environment.sharedAccessGroup()
+        #if os(macOS)
+        // Login keychain ACLs admit one binary; the group also admits the extension.
+        query[kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain as String] = true
         #endif
+        query[kSecAttrAccessGroup as String] = try account.hasPrefix("biometric.") ? self.environment.privateAccessGroup() : self.environment.sharedAccessGroup()
         return query
     }
 
